<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gullah</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Gullah Geechee</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Gullah are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-americans">African Americans</a> of the Lowcountry of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-carolina">South Carolina</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, descended from enslaved West and Central Africans brought to work the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indigo">indigo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> plantations. Relative isolation on the Sea Islands preserved much of their African heritage, including the Gullah <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criollo-people">creole</a> language, crafts such as sweetgrass baskets, foodways and folk beliefs.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gullah.jpg" alt="Gullah" width="320" height="386" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mattstone911 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sweetgrass_Basket_Maker.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
